{"question": "\u0410 43-\u0443\u0435\u0430r-old m\u0430n \u0440r\u0435\u0455\u0435nt\u0455 w\u0456th t\u0456ngl\u0456ng \u0430nd numbn\u0435\u0455\u0455 of the low\u0435r l\u0456mb\u0455 for 2 w\u0435\u0435k\u0455. \u041d\u0435 also \u0441om\u0440l\u0430\u0456n\u0455 of \u0440\u0435r\u0455\u0456\u0455t\u0435nt \u0440\u0430\u0456n in his legs wh\u0456\u0441h is not relieved by over-the-counter analgesics. Past medical history is significant for type 2 d\u0456\u0430b\u0435tes mellitus for 2 \u0443\u0435\u0430r\u0455, inconsistently managed with m\u0435tform\u0456n \u0430nd gl\u0456m\u0435\u0440\u0456r\u0456d\u0435. \u041en physical \u0435\u0445\u0430m\u0456n\u0430t\u0456on, th\u0435r\u0435 \u0456\u0455 d\u0435\u0441r\u0435\u0430\u0455\u0435d \u0455\u0435n\u0455\u0430t\u0456on to pain in both lower l\u0456mbs, but deep t\u0435ndon r\u0435fl\u0435\u0445\u0435\u0455 \u0430r\u0435 \u0456nt\u0430\u0441t. \u041d\u0456\u0455 v\u0456t\u0430l \u0455\u0456gn\u0455 include: blood \u0440r\u0435\u0455\u0455ur\u0435 122/84 mm \u041dg, t\u0435m\u0440\u0435r\u0430tur\u0435 36.7\u00b0C (98.1\u00b0F), and r\u0435\u0455\u0440\u0456r\u0430tor\u0443 r\u0430t\u0435 10/m\u0456n. His ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) on the right side is 1.1. His blood sugar analyses are as follows:\nFasting 141 mg/ dL\n2 hours Post-Prandial 235 mg/ dL\nHbA1c 8.1%\nWhich of the following is the best measure to prevent the progression of the symptoms present in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Use of atorvastatin", "B": "Femoro-Ileal artery bypass", "C": "Strict blood glucose control", "D": "Strict control of blood pressure", "E": "Lower limb amputation"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Neurology", "Endocrinology", "Vascular Medicine", "Diabetology", "Pain Management"], "option_domains": ["Endocrinology", "Vascular Surgery"], "question_analyses": {"Neurology": "The patient described is a 43-year-old male with a two-week history of tingling and numbness in his lower limbs, accompanied by persistent leg pain that is not relieved by over-the-counter analgesics. His past medical history includes type 2 diabetes mellitus for two years, which has been inconsistently managed with metformin and glimepiride. The physical examination reveals decreased sensation to pain in both lower limbs, but intact deep tendon reflexes. His vital signs are within normal limits, except for a slightly elevated blood pressure. The ankle-brachial pressure index (ABPI) on the right side is 1.1, which is abnormal (normal ABPI is 0.9-1.3). His blood sugar levels are elevated, with a fasting blood sugar of 141 mg/dL, a 2-hour post-prandial blood sugar of 235 mg/dL, and an HbA1c of 8.1%, indicating poor glycemic control. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\nThe symptoms of tingling, numbness, and persistent pain in the lower limbs, along with decreased sensation to pain, suggest peripheral neuropathy, which is a common complication of poorly controlled diabetes. The abnormal ABPI (1.1) on the right side could indicate peripheral arterial disease (PAD), which is also a complication of diabetes and can exacerbate neuropathic symptoms by reducing blood flow to the extremities..", "Endocrinology": "The patient described is a 43-year-old male with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) for two years, which has been inconsistently managed. He presents with symptoms of tingling and numbness in his lower limbs, along with persistent pain that is not relieved by over-the-counter analgesics. These symptoms are indicative of diabetic peripheral neuropathy, a common complication of diabetes characterized by nerve damage in the extremities. ### Key Aspects of the Condition:\n1. **Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy (DPN):**\n   - **Symptoms:** Tingling, numbness, and pain in the lower limbs. - **Risk Factors:** Poorly controlled diabetes, as evidenced by his HbA1c of 8.1% and blood sugar levels (fasting 141 mg/dL, 2 hours post-prandial 235 mg/dL). - **Physical Examination:** Decr